Below is a **step‑by‑step “wiring checklist”** for the exact situation you described:

| What you see | Most common cause (when the provider’s own style works) | How to fix it |
|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------|---------------|
| The map loads (basemap tiles appear) but **none of your custom layers draw** – no errors in the console. | **`source-layer` name mismatch** – the vector source you added does contain data, but the layer definitions you wrote refer to a layer name that does not exist in that tileset. | 1️⃣ Pull the TileJSON for the source (or look at the provider’s docs). <br>2️⃣ Read the `vector_layers` array – it lists every valid `source‑layer` name and its attribute fields. <br>3️⃣ In each of your style layers, set `"source-layer": "<exact‑name‑from‑TileJSON>"`. <br>4️⃣ Reload – the layers should now render. |
| The map is completely blank (no basemap, no custom layers). | **Source definition is wrong** – you may have used `tiles` instead of `url`, or omitted required metadata (minzoom/maxzoom, `tileSize` for rasters). | Use the provider’s TileJSON endpoint (`url`) whenever it exists. Example: <br>```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"my‑source\",\n  \"type\": \"vector\",\n  \"url\": \"https://tiles.example.com/tileset.json\"\n}\n``` <br>If you must hand‑wire `tiles`, also add `minzoom`, `maxzoom`, and (for rasters) `tileSize`. |
| Your layers appear **under** the basemap labels, covering street names. | **Layer order** – you added your layers after the style’s symbol layers, so they sit on top of everything. | When you call `map.addLayer(myLayer)` pass the ID of the first symbol layer as the second argument, e.g. <br>```js\nconst firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;\nmap.addLayer(myLayer, firstSymbol);\n``` <br>This injects your layer **just below** the first label layer, preserving the basemap’s text. |
| Hover / click highlighting (`setFeatureState`) never fires. | **Features have no stable `id`** – vector tiles often contain only properties, not an explicit `id`. | Add `promoteId` to the source definition so MapLibre uses a property as the feature id. Example: <br>```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"vector\",\n  \"url\": \"https://tiles.example.com/tileset.json\",\n  \"promoteId\": { \"roads\": \"osm_id\" }\n}\n``` <br>Now `setFeatureState({source:'my-source', sourceLayer:'roads', id:12345}, …)` works. |
| Text labels are missing or appear in a weird font. | **Missing `glyphs` URL** – the style root must point at a glyph PBF service. | Add a `glyphs` property (or copy it from the provider’s style): <br>```json\n\"glyphs\": \"https://tiles.example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf\"\n``` |
| Icons (e.g. point symbols) are missing. | **Missing `sprite` URL** – the style root must point at a sprite sheet. | Add a `sprite` property (or copy it from the provider’s style): <br>```json\n\"sprite\": \"https://tiles.example.com/sprites/basic\"\n``` |
| Network tab shows 200s for tile requests but nothing draws. | **Zoom range mismatch** – you hand‑wired a `tiles` source without `minzoom`/`maxzoom`, so MapLibre asks for zoom levels the tileset doesn’t have (returns empty tiles). | If you must use `tiles`, add the correct range: <br>```json\n\"minzoom\": 0,\n\"maxzoom\": 14\n``` <br>Or, better, switch to `url` so the TileJSON supplies the range automatically. |
| CORS errors in the console. | **Cross‑origin requests blocked** – the tile, glyph, or sprite host does not send `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin`. | Configure the server/CDN to send the header (or use a provider that already does). For PMTiles also add `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Headers: Range`. |

